  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1225 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nLake effect rain/snow showers through Friday:\n\nA deep upper low will persist over the Great Lakes late this \nweek keeping the area in cyclonic flow aloft and cool, northerly\nflow. The 12Z sounding from KGRB indicated deep northerly flow \nthroughout the profile with cold advection throughout the lower \natmosphere. This regime will result in areas of low clouds and \nlake effect rain/snow showers into Friday. Reports of around an \ninch of snow have been received this morning across Vilas \nCounty.\n\nLake effect snow showers will continue intermittently across parts \nof north-central Wisconsin into Friday with around 20 to 40 percent \nchances of at least 2 additional inches of snow. \n\nAcross the western shore of Lake Michigan, some wet snow \naccumulations may also occur across Door or Kewaunee Counties, \nmainly overnight into Friday morning, as the low level temperature \nprofiles cool to support mainly snow. With primarily northerly flow \nin the low levels, there is some uncertainty in whether and to what \nextent any bands become established onshore. As a result, confidence \nremains low (~20%) for accumulations of at least 2 inches. We'll be \nmonitoring trends closely heading into tonight. With the warmer \nground and air temps hovering near freezing, any accumulation would \nbe more likely on grassy/cold surfaces in the absence of heavier \nbands. However, motorists are urged to monitor conditions, \nespecially if traveling along lakeshore areas, and practice winter \ndriving conditions in areas of snow showers, especially being early \nin the winter season. \n\nWarming, mostly dry this weekend into next week:\n\nSurface ridging will work east across the Great Lakes over the \nweekend into early next week as mid level heights build across the \ncentral US. Confidence is high for an extended period of mainly dry \nweather. In addition, temperatures will trend back above \naverage into next week as warm advection develops behind the \ndeparting high. Temperatures are expected into the 50s for much\nof the area by the middle of next week, even as average daytime\nhighs continue to fall through the 40s. The next chances for \nprecipitation will hold off until at least late next week when \nthe overall flow pattern is forecast to become southwesterly and\nmoisture spreads northward.",
